--- NOT SHOWN: the pass path ---
  Every verdict in this cluster is `unknown` because the in-cluster analyzer has
  no model, so this run cannot demonstrate that a HEALTHY release is promoted.
  Read honestly: this proves the gate BLOCKS, not that it discriminates. The
  unit suite covers the pass branch (tests/test_canary_judge.py), but a live
  pass needs a model the cluster can reach.

  Reachability re-measured this run, from inside the pa webhook pod:
    host.docker.internal -> 192.168.65.254   HTTP 200
    192.168.65.254       -> 192.168.65.254   HTTP 200
    host.lima.internal   -> DNS-FAIL
  So the earlier note that the host is "behind the Docker VM gateway and
  unreachable" is WRONG -- pods do reach the host. The actual blockers are:
    (1) MLX is not running (`make local-llm-status` -> MLX-LM :18090 down), and
    (2) MLX and the tool-call proxy bind to 127.0.0.1, so reaching them from the
        cluster needs a bind change (0.0.0.0 = LAN exposure, a user decision).
  With those resolved, ANALYZER_LLM_ENDPOINT=http://host.docker.internal:18091/v1
  on the webhook deployment should light up the pass path.

--- restored ---
  Gate returned to its default-OFF state (terraform apply with no -var) and the
  rollout returned to the stable image, so the documented Phase 4 manual
  promote/abort demo is unchanged.

=== end ===


================================================================================
=== FOLLOW-UP (same day): the pass path — and the defect it exposed ===
================================================================================

The run above could only show the gate BLOCKING, because every verdict was
`unknown`. Closing that gap turned out to require three fixes, each only visible
once the previous one was done. Recorded in order, because the order is the
point: each fix revealed the next problem.

--- fix 1: the analyzer never had a model ---
`llm.endpoint` was in values.yaml from the start, pointing at the host MLX
proxy. Only the router consumed it — and the router is off by default. The
webhook, which runs both the incident analyzer and this release gate, never
received it, so it silently ran the heuristic fallback: confidence 0.0, verdict
`unknown`, every time.

So "the in-cluster analyzer has no model" was wrong. The chart simply never told
it where the model was. Also re-measured: pods DO reach the host
(host.docker.internal -> 200); the earlier "behind the Docker VM gateway,
unreachable" note was incorrect. Real blockers were a stopped MLX and a
127.0.0.1 bind.

After wiring ANALYZER_LLM_ENDPOINT into the webhook (delivered through GitOps,
ArgoCD synced), judgements became real: 2.8s, confidence 0.80.

--- fix 2: the gate was manufacturing the signal it judged ---
And the first real judgement was WRONG:

  POST /canary/judge  (healthy canary)
  {"verdict": "fail", "confidence": 0.8,
   "reason": "P2: The rollouts-demo service is experiencing availability issues
              with its Canary probe, indicating potential deployment problems"}

The AnalysisTemplate had been posting a synthetic `CanaryUnderJudgement` FIRING
alert. That payload asserts a problem, so the analyzer found one — fluently, and
with high confidence. The judge's "no signal → pass" rule was unreachable,
because the gate fabricated the signal before judging it.

A gate that confidently blocks every healthy release is worse than one that
says `unknown`: it looks like it is working, so people stop reading it.

Fix: the caller now sends IDENTITY, not a claim —
  {"canary":{"namespace":"...","podPrefix":"rollouts-demo-<canary-hash>"}}
and the gate queries Alertmanager for what is actually firing against those
pods. `None` (no source / unreachable) and `[]` (looked, quiet) are kept
strictly apart: unobserved is `unknown`, quiet is `pass`.

  POST /canary/judge  (healthy canary, after the fix)
  {"verdict": "pass", "confidence": 1.0,
   "reason": "no alerts firing for rollouts-demo-7ff988b864* in argo-rollouts"}
  0.0s — no inference needed when there is nothing to reason about.

--- fix 3: the alerts it reads were on the wrong timescale ---
With the gate now reading real alerts, a crashlooping canary would STILL have
passed: kube-prometheus-stack's `KubePodCrashLooping` has `for: 15m`, and a
canary lives two or three minutes. The gate would read "no alerts firing" for a
canary that was broken its entire life.

A gate is only as fast as the signals it reads. So the chart now ships a
canary-timescale rule (`CanaryPodRestarting`, for: 30s, interval: 15s, scoped to
the rollout's namespace, `severity: warning` so it stays off the paging path).
It carries `release: monitoring` — without that label kps never loads the rule,
and it would sit there looking installed.

--- LIVE: healthy canary (red), gate ON ---
[15s..180s] phase=Paused step=3   ar=rollouts-demo-5c8c95968d-11 Running
  measurements: value="pass" Successful  x3
  NOT aborted. Held at the indefinite `pause: {}` manual gate — the Phase 4
  demo path is intact — then promoted by hand.

--- LIVE: crashlooping canary (command: exit 1), gate ON ---
[165s] phase=Degraded  ar=rollouts-demo-f84fd685d-12 Failed  -> ABORTED
  measurements:
    value="pass" Successful  20:05:07   <- alert had not fired yet
    value="fail" Failed      20:06:08   <- rule fired, gate read the REAL alert
    value="fail" Failed      20:07:08
  message=Metric "agent-verdict" assessed Failed due to failed (2) > failureLimit (1)
  stable rs (red) 4/4 throughout, Available=True.

  That first `pass` is worth keeping in the record: it shows the gate reading
  state that takes time to develop, not asserting a conclusion up front.

--- what this now proves ---
The gate DISCRIMINATES, which the earlier run could not show:
  healthy      -> pass    -> promotion proceeds to the human gate
  crashlooping -> fail    -> auto-abort, stable preserved
  unobservable -> unknown -> blocked (no alert source, or no model)

--- restored ---
Gate back to default OFF (terraform apply, no -var), PrometheusRule removed with
it, rollout back to stable red 4/4.
